  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA low pressure center will drop south into the forecast area\nthrough dawn and into the coming day. This will also continue \nto usher colder air into the region with high temperatures \nstruggling to get out of the 40s. Northwest flow will allow for\nthe lake to assist with the precip with enhanced rain showers.\nThe morning may feature a flake of snow or two in Forest County\nwith the coldest temperatures there. The stronger gradient will\nhold off till Sunday but the advection through the day will\nstill allow for winds to gust up to 30 knots in some cases. Some\nshowers have the potential to produce heavier amounts as the\nNBM does give a 40% chance of exceeding 0.50\". \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe pressure gradient will tighten up on Sunday on the periphery of \ndeveloping low pressure off to our east. Low level flow in the 925-\n850 mb layer strengthens on the back side of it as well nearing 50 \nknots over the ridges. Dependent on inversion height, exact \ndirection, and potential mixing, Sunday could be gusty across the \narea and especially in the WV ridges. Probability of advisory \ncriteria gusts (>46 mph) reaches up to 70% in Eastern Tucker \nCounty on Sunday. It may be a case that just the far eastern tip\nof the county gets especially gusty as the prevailing flow in \nthe lower levels is westerly and thus favors downsloping to the \neast of our area. Will continue to monitor over the next 24-36 \nhours. In the lowlands, peak gusts being >30 mph are a decent \nbet with probability ranging from 40-80%.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nThe early weekend trough departs on Sunday and surface high \npressure establishes across the Ohio Valley returning mostly \ndry weather into next week with near normal temperatures. Latest\n12 hour ensemble trends have turned a bit more in favor of the \nsolution dominated by a subset of GEFS and Euro ensemble \nmembers in which the surface high is slower to build, and a \nslightly more amplified shortwave trough slides through early on\nMonday. This would return some scattered showers to the \nnorthern part of our area and NBM PoPs have nudged up in this \ndirection. Regardless, ensembles converge to better agreement \nthat the high does become our dominant influence with dry \nweather on Tuesday. The pattern then becomes very deviant into \nthe second half of the week as another trough tries to dig out \nof the Great Lakes and quickly amplify over the eastern half of \nthe country. How deep it is and when it arrives remain unknown, \nbut one would expect that a more unsettled pattern returns with \nsurface low development somewhere downstream. That may be local \nor well off into the Atlantic at this point. Once we get a \nbetter idea of how deep and how progressive the trough will be, \nwe'll be able to start to pin down the potential location and \ntrack of a surface low.",
